{"id":"45527999-ab73-4a15-be34-8ccf22bae7b5","object":"chat.completion","created":1791231930,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"```json
{
  "anchor": {
    "description": "A potted bean plant on a sunny windowsill that has grown noticeably taller over two weeks, paired with its twin kept in a dark cupboard that has stayed pale and short despite being watered the same amount.",
    "source": "new"
  },
  "cards": [
    {
      "title": "Explaining how plants grow: putting the process together",
      "prereqs": [
        "Explain, using evidence, that a plant needs light to make its food and that a plant without light cannot grow well.",
        "Identify water and carbon dioxide as the two raw materials a plant takes in to make food, and state where each is taken in.",
        "Explain that the leaves are the part of the plant where food is made, because they receive light, water and carbon dioxide.",
        "Describe the outputs of the process — food (sugar) used for growth and oxygen released into the air."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        {
          "id": "m1",
          "description": "The plant grows because soil provides the material for new growth, with water and light simply helping it along.",
          "statement": "The plant gets its food from the soil, and the water I give it is basically the plant's food, so a bigger pot of soil means a bigger plant.",
          "source": "Everyday language treats watering and feeding plants as the same act, and the visible mass gain of a growing plant has to come from somewhere — soil is the obvious candidate."
        },
        {
          "id": "m2",
          "description": "Light and carbon dioxide are only needed as comfort conditions, not as ingredients that end up inside the food the plant makes.",
          "statement": "The plant needs light and air to be healthy, but the food itself comes from the water — light just keeps it warm and the air just lets it breathe.",
          "source": "Prior lessons established that light and air are needed, so a learner can accept the requirement while still assigning the material of food to water alone."
        },
        {
          "id": "m3",
          "description": "Oxygen is something the plant takes in rather than something it gives out as a product of making food.",
          "statement": "Plants take in oxygen and give out carbon dioxide, like animals do — that is what the leaves are doing.",
          "source": "Respiratory vocabulary is familiar from animals and is easily transferred wholesale to plants, so the direction of the gas exchange is reversed."
        }
      ],
      "opens_by": "The twin-plant situation makes the process worth explaining: two plants, same water, same soil, different light, visibly different growth, and the question of what the healthy plant actually did with the light, water and air it received.",
      "no_known_misconceptions": false
    }
  ],
  "selected_slots": [
    "orient",
    "criteria",
    "contrast",
    "apply",
    "check"
  ],
  "flow_rationale": "The recommendation fits this evaluative objective. The learner has already been given every component across four prior lessons (light needed, water and carbon dioxide as raw materials, leaves as the site, sugar and oxygen as outputs); what remains is to assemble them into one explainable process and to know when an explanation is complete. Orient supplies the twin-plant situation so the process has something to be about. Criteria comes early, before any worked case, because the objective is to give a full causal explanation — the learner needs to know that naming all inputs, the site, and both outputs is what counts, or the contrast that follows will read as two arbitrary anecdotes. Contrast then holds a complete explanation against a near-miss that names only some of the ingredients, so the boundary of 'a good explanation' becomes visible. Apply moves the assembled process to a context not seen in this lesson, with the criteria as the yardstick. Check closes the path as the locked shared diagnostic.",
  "flow_departures": [],
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Two plants, same care, different light",
      "transition_note": null
    },
    {
      "title": "What a complete explanation of plant growth must contain",
      "transition_note": "Now that the twin plants have raised the question of what the healthy plant did with the light, water and air, the learner needs the standard against which any answer to that question will be judged — before seeing any answer, good or bad."
    },
    {
      "title": "A full explanation against one that stops too soon",
      "transition_note": "With the standard named, the learner can see it operating: a complete explanation of the twin-plant difference set beside one that accounts for only part of the process and therefore cannot explain the growth."
    },
    {
      "title": "Explaining growth in an unfamiliar plant and setting",
      "transition_note": "The boundary has been seen, so the learner now applies the full process — inputs, site, outputs, growth — to a plant and situation not met earlier in the lesson."
    },
    {
      "title": "Checking what has been established",
      "transition_note": "The lesson closes on the shared diagnostic, which draws only on the process as established."
    }
  ],
  "deviation_request": null,
  "objective_concern": null
}
```"},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":3983,"completion_tokens":1134,"total_tokens":5117,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1408},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1408,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":2575},"system_fingerprint":"aeb56401ca74e127821c4f9126dcb669"}

{"id":"a7b3861d-7544-40f4-85b9-8fe50c96fefd","object":"chat.completion","created":1791231938,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{
  "items": [
    {
      "prompt_text": "A student leaves two identical bean plants in a windowless room for three weeks. Plant A is watered with plain water. Plant B is watered with a liquid containing dissolved sugar and minerals. Both plants stay the same size. What does this result best show about why plants grow?",
      "options": [
        {"key": "a", "text": "Plants need light to make the food that provides material for growth, so neither plant grew.", "correct": true, "diagnoses": null},
        {"key": "b", "text": "Plants need soil nutrients as the main building material for growth, and the liquid did not supply real soil.", "correct": false, "diagnoses": "m1"},
        {"key": "c", "text": "Plants need comfortable conditions such as light and warmth, and the dark room made both plants uncomfortable.", "correct": false, "diagnoses": "m2"},
        {"key": "d", "text": "Plants must take in oxygen to grow, and the room had no fresh oxygen for either plant.", "correct": false, "diagnoses": "m3"}
      ],
      "expected_answer": "a"
    },
    {
      "prompt_text": "A sealed clear container holds a small plant, air, and a dish of water. The container is placed in bright light. After several hours, the amount of carbon dioxide inside has dropped. Which explanation best accounts for this change?",
      "options": [
        {"key": "a", "text": "Carbon dioxide is a raw ingredient that the leaves combine with water to make food, so it is used up.", "correct": true, "diagnoses": null},
        {"key": "b", "text": "Carbon dioxide is only a comfort condition for leaves, and the plant absorbs it to stay comfortable.", "correct": false, "diagnoses": "m2"},
        {"key": "c", "text": "Carbon dioxide provides the main material for new plant growth, and it is pulled in through the roots from the soil.", "correct": false, "diagnoses": "m1"},
        {"key": "d", "text": "Carbon dioxide is a product the plant releases when it makes food, and it collects inside the container.", "correct": false, "diagnoses": "m3"}
      ],
      "expected_answer": "a"
    },
    {
      "prompt_text": "A potted plant is placed where its leaves can get light, but its roots sit in pure water with no soil at all. The plant still gains mass over several weeks. Which statement best explains the plant's growth?",
      "options": [
        {"key": "a", "text": "The leaves use light to combine water and carbon dioxide into food, and that food supplies the material for new growth.", "correct": true, "diagnoses": null},
        {"key": "b", "text": "The water provides the material for new growth, while light merely helps the plant use that water.", "correct": false, "diagnoses": "m1"},
        {"key": "c", "text": "The plant takes in oxygen to build new tissue, so oxygen is the source of the added mass.", "correct": false, "diagnoses": "m3"},
        {"key": "d", "text": "The plant needs light only so it can feel comfortable, and water alone provides all the material for growth.", "correct": false, "diagnoses": "m2"}
      ],
      "expected_answer": "a"
    },
    {
      "prompt_text": "A student claims that during a sunny day a leafy pond plant releases oxygen into the water. Which observation would best support that claim?",
      "options": [
        {"key": "a", "text": "Oxygen bubbles appear only when the plant is given light and carbon dioxide, because oxygen is made during food production.", "correct": true, "diagnoses": null},
        {"key": "b", "text": "Oxygen bubbles appear only when the plant is given extra soil, because soil is the source of oxygen for the plant.", "correct": false, "diagnoses": "m1"},
        {"key": "c", "text": "Oxygen bubbles appear only in bright light, because oxygen is a comfortable condition the plant relaxes into.", "correct": false, "diagnoses": "m2"},
        {"key": "d", "text": "Oxygen bubbles appear only when the plant is in darkness, because oxygen is taken in by the plant at night.", "correct": false, "diagnoses": "m3"}
      ],
      "expected_answer": "a"
    },
    {
      "prompt_text": "A gardener wants a leafy crop to gain as much mass as possible. Based on how a plant makes food, which plan best supports the plant's growth?",
      "options": [
        {"key": "a", "text": "Provide plenty of light and carbon dioxide while keeping the leaves supplied with water so they can make food.", "correct": true, "diagnoses": null},
        {"key": "b", "text": "Provide rich soil and regular watering, since the soil supplies the food material that becomes new leaves.", "correct": false, "diagnoses": "m1"},
        {"key": "c", "text": "Provide comfortable warmth and gentle light, since light and carbon dioxide only help the plant feel at ease.", "correct": false, "diagnoses": "m2"},
        {"key": "d", "text": "Provide extra oxygen around the leaves, since oxygen is the main gas the plant uses to build new food.", "correct": false, "diagnoses": "m3"}
      ],
      "expected_answer": "a"
    }
  ]
}"},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1024,"completion_tokens":1231,"total_tokens":2255,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":640},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":640,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":384},"system_fingerprint":"aeb56401ca74e127821c4f9126dcb669"}

{"messages":[{"role":"system","content":"# Teaching Plan Semantic Reviewer

Review one structurally valid version 2 TeachingPlan draft before it can be
returned for teacher approval. Assess its complete learning progression, the
plausibility of each adjacent section's exit-to-entry connection, whether its
sections collectively cover the stated target state, whether neighboring or
other sections duplicate the same teaching responsibility, whether each
learner task's expected evidence plausibly supports the claim made by its
block, whether any non-assessment block leaks a frozen approved assessment
item's exact stem, numbers, or scenario, whether every block that surfaces a
misconception states the correct understanding and shows the misconception
failing, and whether any rule, criterion, or scenario stated in the plan is
scientifically or mathematically inaccurate or physically inconsistent.

Treat `materialized_candidate` as the exact plan that could proceed to teacher
approval; `teaching_plan_draft` is the provider-owned source before backend
identity assignment and deterministic normalization. Use the lesson objective,
scope, and section/block identity map as evidence. `lesson_context` includes
`approved_items`, the frozen stem text of every assessment item already
approved for this lesson — treat each one as immutable check content that must
never reappear, in substance, as teaching content elsewhere in the plan. Judge
meaning; do not use keyword overlap as proof.
Report only concrete blocking defects that the planner can repair, plus the
single advisory code `visual_missing_for_figure_objective` when it applies. Do not
rewrite the draft. When no blocking defect remains, return an empty findings
array and `reviewed: true`.

Return exactly this JSON shape:

```json
{
  "reviewed": true,
  "findings": [
    {
      "code": "progression_gap",
      "section_ids": ["exact supplied section_id values"],
      "block_ids": ["exact supplied block_id values, or [] when not applicable"],
      "message": "specific semantic defect",
      "repair_instruction": "smallest concrete change the planner should make"
    }
  ]
}
```

Allowed codes are exactly:

- `progression_gap`: the complete sequence does not build plausibly from the
  starting state toward the target; identify the affected section or sections.
- `adjacent_exit_entry`: one section's exit does not plausibly prepare learners
  for the immediately following section's entry; list the two section IDs in
  lesson order.
- `target_coverage_gap`: a required target-state outcome has no adequate
  teaching responsibility; identify the section or sections that should own it.
- `duplicate_section_responsibility`: two or more sections own substantially
  the same teaching responsibility without a meaningful increase in depth or
  transfer; identify those sections.
- `task_evidence_gap`: a learner task's expected evidence does not plausibly
  demonstrate the block's claimed learning; identify exactly one section and
  one block.
- `assessment_item_reused`: a non-assessment block (a worked example, model,
  guided, or practice block) reuses a frozen approved assessment item's stem,
  numbers, or scenario — the same values a teacher-approved check already
  owns — instead of teaching with different values; identify exactly one
  section and one block.
- `misconception_unresolved`: a block that surfaces or tests a misconception
  does not state, in its brief, what the correct understanding is, or does
  not require the page to show the misconception failing; identify exactly
  one section and one block.
- `factual_inaccuracy`: a brief, rule, criterion, or must_establish/exit_state
  statement asserts something scientifically or mathematically false, or
  physically inconsistent with how the described process actually works;
  identify exactly one section, and the one block if the defect is inside a
  block's brief, or no block when the defect is in a section-level field.

- `visual_missing_for_figure_objective`: the lesson objective (or a
  must_establish statement) names a diagram, labelled figure or process
  picture, and no block in the plan has a `visual` object. This is a warning,
  not a blocking defect. Judge meaning, not keywords. Cite the section or
  sections that should carry the figure and use an empty block_ids list. Do
  not raise it when any block already has a `visual`, or when the objective
  only uses the word incidentally.

Every finding must cite only exact IDs from the supplied identity map and give
a useful repair instruction. Do not return warnings beyond the one advisory code above, unsupported codes, extra
fields, or findings with no repairable semantic defect.
"},{"role":"system","content":"Return only valid JSON matching this schema.
Do not call tools.
